    <comment>New package: switchyard - NVIDIA's routing and translating proxy for LLM traffic.

Upstream: https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Switchyard, Apache-2.0, Rust + Python.
Packaged at the stable v0.2.0 tag (2026-08-10), not the v0.2.0-rc.* tags.

This SUPERSEDES sr#1371515, which was incomplete: it shipped only the Rust
daemon and left out the command upstream's own README documents. That package
installed /usr/bin/switchyard-server and nothing else, so
&quot;switchyard launch claude --model switchyard&quot; - the documented workflow - could
not be run at all. The `switchyard` command is a console script of upstream's
Python distribution (nemo-switchyard), which the first submission deliberately
excluded. This revision brings it in.

WHAT IS SHIPPED NOW

  switchyard                    the standalone daemon
    /usr/bin/switchyard-server
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/switchyard.service
    /usr/lib/sysusers.d/system-user-switchyard.conf
    /etc/switchyard/            0750 root:switchyard, for config.toml

  python3NN-switchyard          the CLI and the importable library
    /usr/bin/switchyard         via update-alternatives
    .../site-packages/switchyard/          the Python library
    .../site-packages/switchyard_rust/     wrappers + _switchyard_rust.abi3.so
    .../site-packages/nemo_switchyard-0.2.0.dist-info/

WHY THE CLI IS IN THE PYTHON SUBPACKAGE AND WHY THE LINK IS Recommends

/usr/bin/switchyard is a [project.scripts] console script whose import graph is
the Python package itself, so it belongs with that package; with more than one
Python flavour it also has to go through update-alternatives, which only the
flavoured subpackage can drive.

Neither half needs the other, so neither hard-requires the other:

  - switchyard-server is a self-contained Rust daemon that embeds no Python. A
    hard Requires would drag openai, anthropic, fastapi, pydantic, uvicorn and
    the extension module onto a minimal proxy host for nothing.
  - the CLI never execs switchyard-server. There is not one reference to that
    binary anywhere in the Python tree; &quot;switchyard launch&quot; hosts the router
    in-process through the pyo3 binding (switchyard/cli/launchers/
    native_server.py: &quot;from switchyard_rust.server import Server&quot;), and
    docs/cli_reference.md says so explicitly.

They are two independent front ends over the same Rust core. So the main
package carries &quot;Recommends: python3-switchyard&quot;: zypper honours recommends by
default, so &quot;zypper in switchyard&quot; now yields a working `switchyard` command -
the defect is fixed - while --no-recommends and container/appliance builds keep
the daemon free of the Python stack.

HOW THE EXTENSION IS BUILT

maturin, upstream's declared PEP 517 backend, driven through
%pyproject_wheel/%pyproject_install. It is genuinely required rather than a
convenience: `import switchyard` fails closed without the compiled extension
(switchyard_rust/core.py raises when
importlib.import_module(&quot;switchyard_rust._switchyard_rust&quot;) is unavailable), and
the root pyproject.toml's [tool.maturin] manifest-path/module-name/
python-packages keys are what place the cdylib inside the switchyard_rust
package and emit the console script and the nemo-switchyard dist-info that
three modules read through importlib.metadata.version(). A hand-rolled
cargo-build-plus-cp path would have to fabricate all three. The crate builds
from the existing vendor tree with no extra vendoring; it is pyo3
abi3-py312 + extension-module, so the stable-ABI object serves every supported
interpreter.

The ISA-baseline guard is unchanged and now covers maturin too. Upstream's
.cargo/config.toml raises the baseline to x86-64-v3/AVX2 and neoverse-n1;
%cargo_build only neutralises config-file rustflags as a side effect of
exporting a non-empty RUSTFLAGS, and maturin gets no such protection, so %prep
strips any [target.*] table and then asserts that no rustflags survive anywhere
under .cargo/ (a .cargo/config would otherwise shadow .cargo/config.toml), and
%build exports RUSTFLAGS around the maturin call as well.

DEPENDENCIES

The wheel metadata gives openai, anthropic, httpx and pydantic to openSUSE's
Python dependency generator. Declared by hand on top of that:

  fastapi &gt;= 0.136.1, uvicorn &gt;= 0.46.0 - upstream files these under the
      optional &quot;server&quot; extra, but the console script imports
      switchyard.server.server_util at module level and both `serve` and
      `launch` serve over HTTP, so they are mandatory here.
  PyYAML - required and *undeclared upstream*: route bundles are YAML and
      switchyard/cli/route_bundle.py reaches the module through
      import_module(&quot;yaml&quot;), which no metadata scanner can see.

Deliberately not required, with the reasoning recorded in the spec so it is not
&quot;fixed&quot; later: sse-starlette and prompt-toolkit are named in upstream's extras
but nothing in the tree imports either (SSE goes through
fastapi.responses.StreamingResponse; the terminal UI is stdlib pty/termios).
ddtrace is not in Factory at all and redis is pinned &lt;6 while Factory ships
7.0.1 - both are lazily imported behind feature checks that no-op when the
module is absent, so neither blocks anything. The claude/codex/openclaw launch
targets are not dependencies; a missing binary exits with an install hint.

LICENCE

Re-derived over the *two* built targets, since the extension has its own link
closure: switchyard-server 222 crates, switchyard-py 232 (a strict superset).
The ten extra crates are the pyo3 family, pythonize, target-lexicon and two
workspace members - no new identifier, so the tag is unchanged. target-lexicon
is &quot;Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception&quot; and is not declared, because it is
reachable only beneath the pyo3-macros proc-macro crate (a build-time rustc
plugin, never linked into either artifact) and that expression is in any case a
strict subset of the Apache-2.0 obligations already declared. Full method and
the per-identifier accounting are in the spec's Legal-Review-Notice. Each
package that ships a statically linked artifact carries its own deduplicated
copy of the per-crate notice texts, so no symlink crosses a package boundary.

PROJECT MATURITY - please read

The repository was created on 2026-05-19 and the project is at 0.2.0, i.e.
young and pre-1.0. Expect API and configuration churn: the TOML configuration
file carries an explicit &quot;schema_version = 1&quot; field, upstream tags roughly
weekly, and the crates were first published to crates.io on 2026-08-10. I am
submitting it anyway because it is a self-contained daemon plus a leaf Python
package with no reverse dependencies, but a reviewer should know the version
cadence up front.</comment>
